# Real-Time English -> Hindi Voice Translator

A FastAPI WebSocket pipeline: browser mic -> VAD-segmented utterances ->
Whisper ASR -> `gpt-4o-mini` translation -> Edge TTS -> MP3 back to browser.
One `Session` per WebSocket owns five bounded `asyncio.Queue`s and four
worker tasks (ASR, translator, TTS, playback dispatcher).

## Where things live

- `backend/app.py` — FastAPI app + `/ws/audio` endpoint + static mount.
- `backend/session.py` — `Session`: queues, workers, interrupt protocol.
- `backend/vad.py` — `webrtcvad` utterance segmenter.
- `backend/messages.py` — dataclasses passed between queues.
- `backend/workers/{asr,translator,tts}.py` — one async worker per stage.
- `backend/prompts.py` — translation system prompt + few-shots.
- `backend/config.py` — env loading and tunables.
- `frontend/{index.html,app.js,pcm-worklet.js}` — minimal vanilla JS client.

## Non-goals (do not add)

The brief explicitly excluded auth, deployment infra, Kubernetes, databases,
monitoring stacks, and UI polish. Do not introduce them unless asked.

## Universal conventions

- Python 3.11+; use `from __future__ import annotations` in new modules.
- All I/O is `asyncio` — never block the event loop with sync HTTP or
  `time.sleep`. Use `asyncio.sleep` / async OpenAI / async file APIs.
- Use the module logger (`logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)`), never
  `print`, in backend code.
- Keep latency the primary success metric. Target end-to-end is ~1-1.5 s
  from utterance end to first Hindi MP3 chunk.
